  "response": "**Setup:**\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(-|¬D) = 0.95 → P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Bayes' theorem:**\n\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / [P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)]\n\nNumerator: 0.98 × 0.002 = 0.00196\n\nDenominator: 0.00196 + (0.05 × 0.998) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 = 0.037794... = 3.7794...%\n\nRounded to two decimal places: **3.78%**\n\nThis illustrates the classic result that even with a fairly accurate test, screening a low-prevalence population produces mostly false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
